(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-11-06"] = {"mth-11-06-m01":{"idea":"The number i was invented to give square roots to negative numbers. Its one defining rule is i² = −1.","steps":["No real number squared gives a negative answer.","So we define a new number i by the rule i² = −1.","A complex number is a + ib, where a and b are real numbers."],"others":"i = 1/2 is just a fraction, and its square is 1/4. i = −1 says i is real, but a real number cannot square to −1. i² = 1 is true for 1 and −1, not for i.","trap":"Do not mix up i = −1 with i² = −1. The rule is about the square of i.","answer":"i² = −1"},"mth-11-06-m02":{"idea":"Powers of i repeat in a cycle of 4. To find a big power, divide the exponent by 4 and keep the remainder.","steps":["The cycle is i¹ = i, i² = −1, i³ = −i, i⁴ = 1.","Divide 23 by 4: 23 = 4 × 5 + 3. The remainder is 3.","So i²³ = (i⁴)⁵ × i³ = 1 × i³.","i³ = i² × i = −i."],"others":"The value 1 would need a remainder of 0. The value −1 would need a remainder of 2. The value i would need a remainder of 1.","trap":"Quick check: remainder 3 always gives −i.","answer":"−i"},"mth-11-06-m03":{"idea":"Powers of i repeat every 4 steps. When the exponent is a multiple of 4, the power is 1.","steps":["Since i⁴ = 1, write i¹⁰⁰ = (i⁴)²⁵.","100 = 4 × 25, so the remainder is 0.","(i⁴)²⁵ = 1²⁵ = 1."],"others":"The value −1 is i², which needs remainder 2. The value −i is i³, which needs remainder 3. The value i needs remainder 1. None fits a remainder of 0.","answer":"1"},"mth-11-06-m04":{"idea":"In z = a + ib, the real part is a. The imaginary part is the real number b, not ib.","steps":["z = a + ib has two pieces: a and ib.","The real part is a.","The imaginary part is b, the number that multiplies i.","Example: for 3 − 4i, the real part is 3 and the imaginary part is −4."],"others":"The choices that give ib as the imaginary part are wrong, because the letter i is never included. The choice that swaps the two parts is also wrong.","trap":"Imaginary part = b, not ib.","answer":"a and b"},"mth-11-06-m05":{"idea":"The conjugate of a complex number has the same real part and the opposite imaginary part.","steps":["The conjugate of a + ib is a − ib.","Here a = 3 and b = −4.","Change the sign of the imaginary part: −4 becomes +4.","So the conjugate of 3 − 4i is 3 + 4i."],"others":"The choice −3 − 4i changes the real part instead. The choice −3 + 4i changes both signs, which gives the negative of the number. The choice 4 − 3i swaps the two parts.","trap":"Only the sign of the imaginary part changes.","answer":"3 + 4i"},"mth-11-06-m06":{"idea":"A number times its conjugate is always a real number. It equals the square of the modulus.","steps":["(a + ib)(a − ib) = a² − (ib)².","Since i² = −1, (ib)² = −b².","So the product is a² + b².","This is |z|², the square of the distance from the origin."],"others":"The choice a² − b² comes from forgetting that i² = −1. The choices 2ib and 2a are what you get from z − z̄ and z + z̄, not from the product.","trap":"Product gives a² + b². Sum gives 2a.","answer":"a² + b², which is |z|²"},"mth-11-06-m07":{"idea":"We multiply two complex numbers like two brackets. Then we replace i² by −1.","steps":["Expand: (2 + 3i)(1 − 2i) = 2 − 4i + 3i − 6i².","Join the i terms: −4i + 3i = −i.","Replace i² by −1: −6i² = +6.","Total: 2 + 6 − i = 8 − i."],"others":"The choice 2 − 6i forgets the cross terms. The choice 8 + i gets the sign of the i term wrong. The choice −4 − i takes −6i² as −6 instead of +6.","trap":"−6i² becomes +6, not −6.","answer":"8 − i"},"mth-11-06-m08":{"idea":"To find 1/(a + ib), multiply the top and bottom by the conjugate. The bottom then becomes a real number.","steps":["The conjugate of the denominator is a − ib.","Multiply the top and bottom by it: (a − ib)/((a + ib)(a − ib)).","The bottom is a² + b².","So 1/(a + ib) = (a − ib)/(a² + b²)."],"others":"The choice with a + b below has the wrong bottom, since the bottom must be a² + b². The choice with a² − b² below has a wrong sign. The choice (a + ib)/(a² + b²) leaves the top without the conjugate.","trap":"Check with 1/i: a = 0, b = 1 gives −i, which is right.","answer":"(a − ib)/(a² + b²)"},"mth-11-06-m09":{"idea":"The modulus is the distance of the point from the origin. We find it with Pythagoras.","steps":["For a + ib, the modulus is √(a² + b²).","Here a = 3 and b = 4.","√(9 + 16) = √25 = 5."],"others":"The value 25 forgets the square root. The value 7 is a + b, which is not the modulus. The value 1 has no link to this number.","trap":"The modulus is √(a² + b²), not a + b.","answer":"5"},"mth-11-06-m10":{"idea":"The modulus of a product is the product of the moduli.","steps":["Write the numbers in polar form with moduli r₁ and r₂.","Multiplying them multiplies the moduli: r₁r₂.","So |z₁z₂| = |z₁||z₂|.","Check: |2i| = 2 and |3| = 3, and |6i| = 6 = 2 × 3."],"others":"The sum |z₁| + |z₂| is only an upper limit for |z₁ + z₂|. The difference is not right for a product. The quotient |z₁|/|z₂| belongs to |z₁/z₂|.","answer":"|z₁||z₂|"},"mth-11-06-m11":{"idea":"To check a square root, square the answer and see if you get the given number.","steps":["Try 2 + i. Square it: (2 + i)² = 4 + 4i + i².","Replace i² by −1: 4 + 4i − 1 = 3 + 4i.","This is the given number, so 2 + i is a square root.","The other square root is −(2 + i)."],"others":"(1 + 2i)² = −3 + 4i. (3 + 2i)² = 5 + 12i. (2 − 2i)² = −8i. None of them gives 3 + 4i.","trap":"Squaring a guess is the fastest test.","answer":"2 + i"},"mth-11-06-m12":{"idea":"This theorem says how many roots a polynomial has in the complex numbers.","steps":["A polynomial of degree n has exactly n roots in the complex numbers.","A repeated root is counted as many times as it repeats.","So a quadratic has 2 roots and a cubic has 3."],"others":"Having one root only is false, since degree n gives n roots. Having no roots above degree 2 is false, since a cubic always has 3. The choice with no complex roots is wrong, because the theorem is about complex roots.","answer":"exactly n roots in the complex numbers, counted with their multiplicity"},"mth-11-06-m13":{"idea":"If a polynomial has real coefficients, its non-real roots come in conjugate pairs.","steps":["The coefficients are real, and 2 + 3i is a root.","So its conjugate is also a root: 2 − 3i.","Check: sum of roots = 4, product = 4 + 9 = 13.","The quadratic is x² − 4x + 13 = 0, which has real coefficients."],"others":"The choices −2 − 3i and −2 + 3i are not conjugates of 2 + 3i. The choice 3 + 2i swaps the two parts. With any of these, the sum or product of the roots would not be real.","trap":"The conjugate flips only the sign of the imaginary part.","answer":"2 − 3i"},"mth-11-06-m14":{"idea":"The Argand diagram is a plane. The real part goes on the horizontal axis and the imaginary part on the vertical axis.","steps":["Write the number as a + ib.","The horizontal axis is the real axis, so a is the x-coordinate.","The vertical axis is the imaginary axis, so b is the y-coordinate.","So a + ib is the point (a, b)."],"others":"The point (ia, ib) puts i inside the coordinates, which must be real. The point (a, 0) ignores b. The point (b, a) swaps the axes.","answer":"the point (a, b), with the real part on the horizontal axis"},"mth-11-06-m15":{"idea":"To find the argument, find the quadrant first. Then find the reference angle.","steps":["The point is (−1, √3). The real part is negative and the imaginary part is positive.","So the point is in the second quadrant.","The reference angle is tan⁻¹(√3/1) = π/3.","The argument is π − π/3 = 2π/3 (120°)."],"others":"The value π/3 is only the reference angle, right for the first quadrant. The values −π/3 and −2π/3 point below the real axis, but our point is above it.","trap":"Do not stop at tan⁻¹. Always check the quadrant.","answer":"2π/3"},"mth-11-06-m16":{"idea":"The polar form writes a complex number with its distance r and its angle θ.","steps":["The real part is r cos θ.","The imaginary part is r sin θ.","So z = r cos θ + i r sin θ = r(cos θ + i sin θ).","Example: 1 + i has r = √2 and θ = π/4."],"others":"The form with sin θ first and cos θ second swaps the two functions. The form r cos θ + sin θ lacks i and r on the second term. The form with a minus sign is the conjugate, with angle −θ.","answer":"r(cos θ + i sin θ)"},"mth-11-06-m17":{"idea":"De Moivre's theorem gives powers of a complex number in polar form. The modulus gets a power and the angle gets multiplied.","steps":["Take z = r(cos θ + i sin θ).","The modulus r is raised to the power n: rⁿ.","The angle θ is multiplied by n: nθ.","So zⁿ = rⁿ(cos nθ + i sin nθ)."],"others":"The choice with cosⁿθ and sinⁿθ puts the power on the trig functions, not on the angle. The choice with nr multiplies the modulus instead of raising it. The choice with r alone forgets to raise r to n.","trap":"Power on r, multiplication on θ.","answer":"[r(cos θ + i sin θ)]ⁿ = rⁿ(cos nθ + i sin nθ)"},"mth-11-06-m18":{"idea":"Use polar form and De Moivre's theorem to raise 1 + i to a high power.","steps":["For 1 + i: modulus = √2, argument = π/4.","(1 + i)⁸ = (√2)⁸ [cos 8(π/4) + i sin 8(π/4)].","(√2)⁸ = 16, and 8(π/4) = 2π.","cos 2π = 1 and sin 2π = 0, so the answer is 16."],"others":"The values 8i and 256 come from wrong powers of the modulus or angle. The value −16 would need the angle to be π, not 2π. Check: (1 + i)² = 2i, and (2i)⁴ = 16.","trap":"(√2)⁸ = 2⁴ = 16, not 256.","answer":"16"},"mth-11-06-m19":{"idea":"The three cube roots of unity are 1, ω and ω². Their sum is zero.","steps":["ω is a root of ω³ = 1, with ω ≠ 1.","Factor: ω³ − 1 = (ω − 1)(1 + ω + ω²) = 0.","Since ω ≠ 1, the factor ω − 1 is not zero.","So 1 + ω + ω² = 0."],"others":"The values ω, −1 and 1 are not the sum. Test with ω = (−1 + i√3)/2: ω² = (−1 − i√3)/2, and 1 + ω + ω² = 1 − 1/2 − 1/2 = 0.","trap":"The sum of the cube roots of unity is zero.","answer":"0"},"mth-11-06-m20":{"idea":"Since ω³ = 1, the powers of ω repeat every 3 steps.","steps":["Divide 100 by 3: 100 = 3 × 33 + 1.","ω¹⁰⁰ = (ω³)³³ × ω.","(ω³)³³ = 1, so the result is ω."],"others":"The value 1 would need a remainder of 0. The value ω² would need a remainder of 2. The value −1 is not a power of ω at all.","trap":"The remainder after dividing by 3 decides the answer.","answer":"ω"},"mth-11-06-m21":{"idea":"We use 1 + ω + ω² = 0 to rewrite 1 + ω. Then we cube it.","steps":["From 1 + ω + ω² = 0, we get 1 + ω = −ω².","Cube both sides: (1 + ω)³ = (−ω²)³ = −ω⁶.","ω⁶ = (ω³)² = 1.","So the result is −1."],"others":"The value +1 loses the minus sign. The value 0.0 would need 1 + ω = 0, which is false. The value ω² would come from stopping before cubing.","trap":"Keep the minus sign when you cube −ω².","answer":"−1"},"mth-11-06-m22":{"idea":"|z − a| is the distance of z from the point a. Equal distances from two points give the perpendicular bisector.","steps":["|z − 1| is the distance of z from 1. |z + 1| is the distance from −1.","They are equal, so z is equidistant from 1 and −1.","With z = x + iy: (x − 1)² + y² = (x + 1)² + y².","This simplifies to −2x = 2x, so x = 0, the imaginary axis."],"others":"The real axis is y = 0, which is not equidistant. The line x = 1 passes through one of the points only. The unit circle is the set of points at distance 1 from the origin, not equal distance from two points.","answer":"the imaginary axis, which is the perpendicular bisector of the points 1 and −1"},"mth-11-06-m23":{"idea":"|z − z₀| = r describes all points at distance r from z₀. That is a circle.","steps":["Write |z − 2| = 3.","The centre is the point 2, that is (2, 0).","The radius is 3.","In coordinates, (x − 2)² + y² = 9."],"others":"A centre at the origin would need |z| = 3. A centre at −2 would need |z + 2| = 3. A line is not a set of points at a fixed distance from one point.","trap":"In |z − z₀|, the centre is z₀, with the sign changed.","answer":"a circle with the centre at the point 2 and the radius 3"},"mth-11-06-m24":{"idea":"The distance between two points z₁ and z₂ is |z₁ − z₂|.","steps":["Subtract: (1 + 2i) − (4 + 6i) = −3 − 4i.","The modulus is √((−3)² + (−4)²).","√(9 + 16) = √25 = 5."],"others":"The value 25 forgets the square root. The value 7 adds the differences, 3 + 4. The value √7 comes from 16 − 9, a wrong sign.","trap":"Distance uses a sum of squares under the root.","answer":"5"},"mth-11-06-m25":{"idea":"Use the quadratic formula. A negative discriminant gives a conjugate pair.","steps":["Here a = 1, b = 2, c = 5.","Discriminant = 4 − 20 = −16, and √(−16) = 4i.","z = (−2 ± 4i)/2.","So z = −1 ± 2i."],"others":"The pair −2 ± i forgets to divide the 4i by 2. The pair −1 ± 4i makes the same mistake. The pair 1 ± 2i has the wrong sign for the real part. Check: the sum of the roots is −2, which matches −b/a.","trap":"Divide the whole numerator by 2a, both parts.","answer":"−1 + 2i and −1 − 2i"},"mth-11-06-n01":{"idea":"We multiply two complex numbers like brackets, then ask for the real part.","steps":["Expand: (2 + 3i)(1 − 2i) = 2 − 4i + 3i − 6i².","Use i² = −1: −6i² = +6.","Real parts: 2 + 6 = 8. Imaginary parts: −4i + 3i = −i.","The product is 8 − i, so the real part is 8."],"trap":"Check: the answer is a whole number, as asked.","answer":"8"},"mth-11-06-n02":{"idea":"To divide complex numbers, multiply the top and bottom by the conjugate of the bottom. Then take the modulus.","steps":["The conjugate of 1 − 2i is 1 + 2i. The bottom becomes (1 − 2i)(1 + 2i) = 1 + 4 = 5.","The top is (3 + 4i)(1 + 2i) = 3 + 6i + 4i − 8 = −5 + 10i.","The quotient is (−5 + 10i)/5 = −1 + 2i.","Modulus = √(1 + 4) = √5 = 2.236.","Check: |3 + 4i|/|1 − 2i| = 5/√5 = √5."],"answer":"2.236"},"mth-11-06-n03":{"idea":"To find a square root of a + ib, guess a form x + iy and match real and imaginary parts.","steps":["Let (x + iy)² = 3 + 4i. Then x² − y² = 3 and 2xy = 4.","From 2xy = 4, y = 2/x. Put it in: x² − 4/x² = 3.","So x⁴ − 3x² − 4 = 0, which gives x² = 4 and x = 2.","Then y = 1, so the root is 2 + i.","The real part is 2.000."],"trap":"Check: (2 + i)² = 4 + 4i − 1 = 3 + 4i.","answer":"2.000"},"mth-11-06-n04":{"idea":"The argument is the angle of the point from the positive real axis. Find the quadrant first.","steps":["The point is (−1, √3), in the second quadrant.","The reference angle is tan⁻¹(√3/1) = 60°.","Argument = 180° − 60° = 120°.","So the answer is 120.00°."],"trap":"Do not give 60°. That is the angle for the first quadrant.","answer":"120.00°"},"mth-11-06-n05":{"idea":"Use polar form and De Moivre's theorem for a high power.","steps":["1 + i has modulus √2 and argument 45° (π/4).","(1 + i)⁸ = (√2)⁸ [cos 8 × 45° + i sin 8 × 45°].","(√2)⁸ = 16 and 8 × 45° = 360°.","cos 360° = 1 and sin 360° = 0, so the value is 16.000."],"trap":"Check: (1 + i)² = 2i, and (2i)⁴ = 16.","answer":"16.000"},"mth-11-06-n06":{"idea":"We use 1 + ω + ω² = 0 and ω³ = 1 to simplify (1 + ω)³.","steps":["From 1 + ω + ω² = 0, we get 1 + ω = −ω².","(1 + ω)³ = (−ω²)³ = −ω⁶.","ω⁶ = (ω³)² = 1² = 1.","So (1 + ω)³ = −1, a real number. The real part is −1.000."],"trap":"Check: 1 + ω = 0.5 + 0.866i has modulus 1 and angle 60°. Cubing makes the angle 180°, which is −1.","answer":"-1.000"},"mth-11-06-n07":{"idea":"The distance between two points in the Argand diagram is the modulus of their difference.","steps":["Difference: (1 + 2i) − (4 + 6i) = −3 − 4i.","Distance = √((−3)² + (−4)²).","= √(9 + 16) = √25 = 5.000."],"trap":"Check: the points are (1, 2) and (4, 6), a 3-4-5 triangle.","answer":"5.000"},"mth-11-06-n08":{"idea":"Solve the quadratic with the formula. Then take the modulus of a root.","steps":["For z² + 2z + 5 = 0: a = 1, b = 2, c = 5.","Discriminant = 4 − 20 = −16, so √(−16) = 4i.","z = (−2 ± 4i)/2 = −1 ± 2i.","Modulus = √(1 + 4) = √5 = 2.236.","Both roots are conjugates, so they have the same modulus."],"trap":"Check: the product of the roots is c/a = 5, and 2.236² = 5.","answer":"2.236"},"mth-11-06-n09":{"idea":"The principal argument lies between −180° and 180°. Find the quadrant, then the reference angle.","steps":["The point is (−2, −2), in the third quadrant.","The reference angle is tan⁻¹(2/2) = 45°.","In the third quadrant, the principal argument is −(180° − 45°) = −135°.","So the answer is −135.00°."],"trap":"Do not give 225°. That is the same direction but outside the principal range.","answer":"-135.00°"},"mth-11-06-n10":{"idea":"A number times its conjugate gives the square of its modulus.","steps":["z = 3 − 4i, so z̄ = 3 + 4i.","z z̄ = (3 − 4i)(3 + 4i) = 9 − 16i².","Use i² = −1: 9 + 16 = 25.","This equals |z|² = 3² + 4² = 25."],"answer":"25"}};
